INT. NIGHT BAKERY, BACK KITCHEN - 3 A.M.

Steel tables dusted white. Proofing racks glow under a single caged bulb. A radio murmurs a late jazz station. Flour hangs in the air like weather.

SOLEDAD VEGA, 30s, forearms floured to the elbow, shapes loaves with the speed of someone who has done it ten thousand times. ARJUN MEHTA, 30s, in a good coat that doesn't belong here, stands at the back door holding a small box he hasn't opened.

BEA LINDGREN, 20s, the new apprentice, sits on an upturned crate peeling a clementine, trying to look invisible.

                    SOLEDAD
          Door's open. You're letting the heat out.

                    ARJUN
          I wanted to say, I mean, I came to say

                    BEA
          I can go. I should go, actually, I have a

                    SOLEDAD
          Stay, Bea.

                    ARJUN
          Please, Bea, it's fine. It's

                    SOLEDAD
          It's not fine. It's three in the morning.

Arjun steps in and shuts the door. He sets the box on the table beside a tray of rolls, and Soledad slides it two inches away from the dough without looking at it. Bea tears the clementine in half and offers him a piece, which he takes automatically, which makes Soledad laugh once through her nose.

                    ARJUN
          I kept the key. I'm sorry. I should've mailed it.

                    BEA
          I'm going to check the oven.

                    SOLEDAD
          The oven is fine.

                    BEA
          I'm going to check it anyway.

She leaves through the swing door. It flaps twice behind her. The radio drops into a slow saxophone.

                    ARJUN
          You cut your hair.

                    SOLEDAD
          Eight months ago.

                    ARJUN
          It suits you.

                    SOLEDAD
          Don't.

He opens the box. Inside is a brass key on a loop of red thread. He sets it carefully next to the bread, as though it might wake the dough. She wipes her hands on her apron, one finger at a time, then picks up the key and drops it in the flour bin without looking.

                    ARJUN
          Soledad

                    SOLEDAD
          The seven o'clock rush is in four hours. I've got six hundred rolls.

                    BEA (O.S.)
          (calling)
          Oven's fine!

                    SOLEDAD
          (calling back)
          Told you!

Arjun nods, takes a roll off the tray, eats half of it standing there. Flour dusts his lapel. He lets it.

                    ARJUN
          Still the best thing in the city.

                    SOLEDAD
          It's flour and water.

                    ARJUN
          It's you.

She takes the other half out of his hand and finishes it herself, chewing slowly, looking at the wall clock. Then she holds the swing door open for him.
